TY  - JOUR
TI  - Screening and Testing of the Headache Patient
PY  - 2015
JO  - Headache and Migraine Biology and Management
AB  - In clinical practice, the majority of patients who present with headache have normal physical and neurologic examinations. For most of these patients, the diagnosis of primary headache disorders can be established without supplemental testing. However, it is necessary to distinguish between primary and secondary headache disorders. The decision to proceed with neuroimaging largely depends on clinical presentations that warrant suspicion for a secondary headache disorder. Lumbar puncture is warranted when an infectious etiology, such as meningitis or encephalitis, is suspected, or to evaluate for subarachnoid hemorrhage or pseudotumor cerebri. Electroencephalography (EEG) is not recommended for the evaluation of the headache patient unless there is associated seizure activity, or to distinguish between an actual seizure disorder and psychogenic non-epileptic seizures. Clinical laboratory studies are generally not helpful in the diagnosis of common headache disorders but can be used for evaluation of possible secondary headache disorders, such as erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) for giant cell arteritis. Genetic testing for headache disorders is not common.
