PY  - 2014
JO  - Acta Medica Mediterranea
AB  - Aims: The aim of this study was to assess the value of National Institutes of Health Stroke Scale Score (NIHSS) + ABCD2 score and to evaluate its efficacy in diagnosis of Cerebrovascular disease (CVD) at the emergency department admission. Materials and methods: The patients who had functional neurological disorders admitted to the study. NIHSS at presentation determined before imaging protocol. Final stroke diagnose was made by MRI. Detected in DWI, lesion location, number and size in mL were calculated by radiologist. ABCD2 score points added to NIH score. NIHSS + ABCD2 score were used as the primary measurement to determine the location of ischemia and right hemisphere and left hemisphere lesion. Results: The 130 patients were admitted to the study and 64.6% of the patients were diagnosed with CVD. The median NIH score was 4 (IQR: 2-8). NIHSS+ABCD2 sensitivity was higher than NIHSS for the diagnose of CVD. Lesion volumes were 1.87mL (IQR 0.74 - 14.1) for left hemisphere and were 5.4mL (IQR 0.97 - 20.8) for right hemisphere. The median NIHSS + ABCD2 score was 8.73 (95%Cl, 6.99-10.47) for left hemisphere involvement and was 9.11 (95%Cl, 6-10.92) for right hemisphere involvement. NIHSS + ABCD2 sensitivity for right hemisphere lesions was higher than left hemisphere lesions. There was a positive correlation between NIHSS and the DWI lesion volume. Conclusion: Our study showed that the attachment of ABCD2 to NIH score before performing MR, could be used to determine prospect CVD patients at ED, increased the sensitivity of NIHSS score. Nevertheless, it did not change PPV and NPV volumes, and has no clinical contribution in prediction of CVD diagnosis.
